To find the minimum of $f(x),$ we take its derivative using the quotient rule:
\begin{align*}
f'(x) &= \frac{\sqrt{\frac{1}{x - 1}} - \frac{1}{2} \cdot \frac{9}{(x - 1)^{\frac{3}{2}}}}{\left( (x - 1)^{1/2} \right)^2} \\
&= \frac{\frac{2}{2\sqrt{x - 1}} - \frac{9}{2(x - 1)^{\frac{3}{2}}}}{x - 1} \\
&= \frac{2(x - 1) - 9}{2(x - 1)^{\frac{3}{2}}}
\end{align*}

Setting this equal to $0,$ we solve $\frac{2(x - 1) - 9}{2(x - 1)^{\frac{3}{2}}} = 0,$ so $\boxed{x = \frac{11}{2}}$ is the only critical point, which is in the domain of $f.$

We use the second derivative test to classify this point. We compute $f''(x) = \frac{-3(25 - 4x)}{2(x - 1)^{\frac{7}{2}}}.$ Because $f''\!\left( \frac{11}{2} \right) < 0,$ we conclude that $f$ has a maximum at $x = \frac{11}{2}.$ Then $\boxed{f_{\text{min}} = \frac{51}{\sqrt{3}}.}$

